"The postoperative pathology reveals a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3) and pathological stage pT2a. A positive surgical margin was documented, which is an adverse finding. Countervailing findings include the absence of extraprostatic extension and no invasion of the seminal vesicles, as noted in the surgical pathology report: \"There was no extraprostatic extension; surgical margins were positive; the seminal vesicles were not invaded; lymphovascular invasion was absent; there was no lymph node metastasis.\" The biopsy showed a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 2). Nodal status is documented as \"no lymph node metastasis.\" The absence of lymphovascular invasion and seminal vesicle invasion are reassuring features. Prognosis is complicated by the positive surgical margins. Postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are needed, as \"pathology alone does not establish biochemical recurrence.\" Missing postoperative PSA information widens the uncertainty regarding biochemical control. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory would be crucial in revising the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 46.70 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 35.03–58.38 months; its coverage has not been validated."
